From d0c43e1650dad9af7b8e14b5fc1fcd2d0e5f2a5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 02:49:44 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 44/45] WIP: ASoC: ti: samsung-espresso: add headset jack
 detection

The Galaxy Tab 2 detects the headphone jack with a switch on a GPIO
(DET_3_5) and headset keys with a second one (EAR_SEND_END). Which of
headphones or a 4-pole headset is plugged in, and which key is pressed,
comes from the voltage of the headset microphone line, read with an ADC
while the headset mic bias is on: an STMPE811 channel on the 10.1".

Add optional jack detection as in the Midas driver: headset-detect-gpios,
headset-key-gpios, the "headset-detect" IIO channel and the 4-pole and
key thresholds (in mV, as on Midas despite the property names), and the
binding for them.

Unlike on Midas, the key line here is a comparator on the microphone
line and only works with the bias on: with the bias off it reads
"pressed". So keep the bias on while a 4-pole headset is plugged in
(as the vendor kernel does), and turn it off for headphones and on
removal. The line also takes about 100 ms to settle after the bias comes
on (20 ms gave half the final voltage), so read it until it is stable.
And while a plug is still going in, the jack switch already closes but
the microphone contact doesn't touch yet: the line then sits at its
unloaded level (about 2770 mV, as with the jack empty) for a few hundred
ms before dropping to the headset's level (about 2080 mV). Keep reading
while it reads above the 4-pole window, for up to 2 s.
On removal, release a key that was still reported as pressed, and ignore
the key line once the jack switch reads empty.

Tested on a GT-P5113 with earbuds (4-pole, one key): insertion, removal
and KEY_MEDIA presses; with the bias turned off after detection (as on
Midas) the key got stuck, and a slow insertion was classified on the
unloaded plateau.

WIP: the binding is still in this commit.

Assisted-by: LLM
